      "id": 1,
      "text": "Caraquet was founded around 1731 by Breton trader Gabriel Giraud dit Saint‑Jean.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caraquet"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Mi’kmaq term meaning “meeting of two rivers,” referring to the Caraquet and Rivière du Nord.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caraquet"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1757, Alexis Landry led settlers to establish the original town site at what is now Sainte‑Anne‑du‑Bocage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caraquet"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Acadian Historic Village, open June‑September, offers interpretive trails and historic buildings that let visitors walk through 200 years of Acadian life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.todocanada.ca/experience-the-heart-of-acadian-culture-in-caraquet-heritage-tours-oyster-tastings-seaside-dining-and-coastal-charm/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Caraquet hosts the annual Acadian Festival each August, climaxing with the Tintamarre parade on August 15.",
